@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-applypatch-semantics-atomic-no-mutation-root-removal-is-an-error
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyPatch semantics: atomic, no mutation, root removal is an error"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 leaves some behaviour open (whole-document removal, error handling, equality details). `applyPatch`
+is used by services on untrusted patches, and must not mutate its input.
+
+## Decision
+
+- The document and every `value` are deep-copied (rejecting non-JSON values); operations run on the copy,
+  so the input is never mutated and a failing patch is atomic (it throws, returns nothing).
+- Pointers are parsed locally (the `-` token is valid for `add` only), not through `resolvePointer`, which rejects `-`.
+- Strictness follows dl-001: own properties only, array indices without leading zeros; `add` accepts an index equal to the length.
+- Removing or moving away the whole document (`remove` with path `""`) throws, since a document cannot be absent.
+- `move` into a child of its own `from` throws; `add`/`replace` with path `""` replace the document.
+- `test` uses JSON equality: object member order is ignored, numbers and types compared strictly.
+- Missing/unknown `op`, missing `path`/`from`/`value`, or a non-array patch throws; extra members are ignored.
+- Members are set with `defineProperty`, so a `__proto__` key is plain data (no prototype pollution).
+
+## Consequences
+
+Safe to call with untrusted input; callers get all-or-nothing results. Error types are plain `Error`.
+Documents with `undefined`, functions or non-finite numbers are rejected.
